  "- [ ] `evolution/experiment-lifecycle` Implement experiment records, exposure and expiry decisions\n\nStatus: Open\n\nSource requirement: `governance/experiment-lifecycle`\n\nRequired outputs: Owned experiment registry entries and explicit opt-in artifact metadata On/off fixtures, expiry-check result, reviewed extension/stabilization/removal decision and lifecycle notes Per-use admission/refusal record bound to the experiment, explicit opt-in source/package/compiler/build metadata, supported profile/channel, compatibility-safe stable adapter and applicable specialist review; downstream export manifests identify experimental stability.\n\nClosing evidence: Record owning document, scope/state, profiles, default, expiry, rollback and stabilization/removal criteria; stable default is off except a governed preview decision. Compiler/package/build use records opt-in; stable consumers require compatibility-safe isolation and sensitive exposure first obtains GOV4/GOV9/GOV10 review. Actual expiry tooling checks an expired entry and emits reviewed extension, stabilization or removal with diagnostic/opt-out guidance; disabled fixtures preserve stable behavior. Actual complete accepted artifact and consumer result for the named source contract, with input/source/policy/tool identity. Historical stage0 records or a passing schema alone do not implement these outcomes. Before experimental use, check the owned experiment record, profile availability, default/preview channel, explicit opt-in and nonexpired reviewed state; never expose proposed/rejected/removed experiments as active. Stable modules and exports reject unisolated experimental dependence. Record opt-in in actual source/package/compiler/build artifacts, preserve the experiment-on/off fixture inputs and emit GOV7001-GOV7008 and STD20002 as applicable with replacement or opt-out guidance. For standard-library exports consume the declared stability entry so experimental/internal exports cannot masquerade as stable imports; package tooling rejects internal experimental exports or experimental exports lacking required opt-in.\n\nRequired rejection evidence: Reject ownerless/unscoped records, silent stable dependence, unrecorded opt-in, unreviewed default-on exposure and missing rollback/expiry/exit criteria. Reject continuing expired experiments without a reviewed decision, sensitive exposure without specialist review and stabilization without GOV8 evidence. Malformed/missing/stale/wrong-scope records and each named source rejection produce the original GOV diagnostics at the actual consuming boundary. Reject missing owner/document/profile/expiry/rollback, unapproved stable default-on exposure, missing explicit opt-in or artifact record, stable dependence without isolation, missing relevant GOV4/GOV9/GOV10 review, expired use without reviewed extension/stabilization/removal, and stabilization without GOV8 evidence. Reject absent or misleading export stability metadata and forbidden internal experimental imports.\n\nDependencies: GOV1 change and GOV6 RFC; GOV2 stable boundary classification; GOV4/GOV9/GOV10 sensitive exposure review; GOV8 exit decision; Compiler/build/package on/off fixtures and release metadata. Dependencies are required artifacts/conditional consumers, not a claim their producer tasks are implemented or a cyclic all-tasks-complete gate. Experiment lifecycle producer supplies owned state/expiry/rollback and reviewed extension/exit; library-admission supplies module/export stability metadata; actual compiler/package/build owners supply opt-in artifact and on/off conformance results; GOV2 compatibility-safe isolation and relevant GOV4/GOV9/GOV10 exposure decisions remain separately produced inputs. The governance checker consumes these artifacts and does not implement all downstream compilers or package tools.\n\nExact source criteria: facet/bootstrap-governance/bf581f080f0835efdc348a2b, facet/bootstrap-governance/e466709753bdcaebd5421308, facet/bootstrap-governance/1