  "### Dependency stages and finite acceptance\n\nThe phase `Dependencies:` declarations below identify required interfaces and\nexecutable composition evidence. They do not require every upstream task's\nfinal checkbox before construction or integration can begin. Reading them as\nfinal-checkbox prerequisites creates the actual strongly connected sets\ndocumented in [the dependency audit](acceptance-dependencies.md#source-grounded-cycles).\nUse the following stages for every existing task ID; the ID, complete task\nscope, proof gates and recorded task status remain unchanged.\n\n| Stage | Required evidence and owner | Prerequisites |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Interface | Owning component's normative input/output, semantic facts, diagnostics and fixture contract. A document is sufficient only for this construction interface. | Existing canonical contracts; no downstream task completion. |\n| Implementation | Owning implementation executes real accepted and rejected cases for its declared increment. Pinned fixtures may stand in for unfinished producers only at this stage, with that limitation explicit. | Its interface and the declared dependency interfaces. |\n| Composition | The consuming component executes actual compatible producer implementations for every declared dependency on the exact candidate closure, independently checks required facts and rejects relevant substitutions, stale identities and malformed products. | Its implementation and the dependency implementations; a producer's final release checkbox is not the evidence. |\n| Acceptance | Independent review accepts the task's entire original scope and unchanged proof gates, with exact source/compiler/fixture/target/runtime identities and residual boundaries. Partial implementation or composition leaves the task open. | Its composition and all task-specific final proof requirements. |\n\nThese are evidence stages, not four successor implementation tasks or a new\nstatus ledger. Every original dependency survives at composition. An interface,\nsynthetic carrier, saved report, structural validator or historical receipt\ncannot satisfy that stage. Where an upstream task remains open, acceptance\nmust independently establish the particular executable upstream contract\nconsumed by this task; uncertainty leaves acceptance NOT ESTABLISHED.\n\nConformance-harness construction (`FL-P14-T01`) consumes declared fixture and\ndiagnostic interfaces. Its full-population acceptance still requires actual\nfeature execution; features requiring the harness consume its executable\nimplementation, not a claim of already completed full conformance.\n`FL-P14-T02` runner implementation is available to the next-stage rebuild;\nits final self-hosted-public-binary proof is evaluated on that actual rebuilt\ncandidate, alongside `FL-P15-T02` and the final Phase 18 gates. None of these\ntasks closes on the strength of runner construction alone.\n\nLikewise, Backend/Runtime, Schema/Build, Math/Compiler/Performance and\nGovernance/Release each construct against explicit upstream interfaces, then\nverify the real joint candidate. Governance rejection rules must execute before\nfinal release acceptance. Public-binary and reproducible-rebuild claims still\nrequire the existing final suite, diagnostics, provenance, signing, SBOM,\nreview and genuine seed-retirement evidence. No safety check is postponed.\n\nThe read-only `tools/acceptance_dependencies.clj --check` command extracts the\n36-task dependency graph and the separate 30-milestone completion graph,\nreports their strongly connected sets, and checks the staged prerequisite\ngraph. It verifies structure only, never execution or completion. See the\n[finite cleanup contract](acceptance-dependencies.md) for its tested boundary.\n\nWorkers should execute one task at a time in dependency order. If a later phase\nneeds an early slice, it may proceed only against documented interfaces and may\nnot claim full completion before the dependency proof exists.\n\n1. Build a machine-readable coverage matrix from every normative document to\n   implementation modules, accepted fixtures, rejected fixtures, diagnostics,\n   artifacts, and proof commands.\n2. Replace generic public-binary unsupported-source outcomes with the real\n   compiler path for the next narrow language slice.\n3. Move implementation ownership from Clojure into Gravity-authored compiler,\n   runtime, and standard-library sources.\n4. Keep the Clojure bootstrap as a shrinking seed and audit path until the\n   self-hosted binary proves it is outside product behavior.\n5. After each slice, run `clojure -M:test`,\n   `clojure -M tools/validate_gravity_docs.clj`, `git diff --check`, and the\n   capability-specific proof commands.\n6. Record evidence only after the commands pass.\n\n",
  "e72027e8febb7e7f5c9014835f2717d006ce664ce1fb091cc54b47f2efe3800a"
  "## Three boundaries and one shared completion rule\n\nFollow [development verification](development-verification.md), using existing\ncompiler interfaces and direct tests:\n\n| Boundary | What is sufficient for that boundary | What it does not establish |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Implementation | Owning implementation changes; real accepted and rejected cases; focused semantic and preservation checks. | Authoritative module migration or milestone completion. |\n| Integration | Affected producer-to-consumer composition, interface, diagnostic and regression checks. | Whole-stage equivalence, all targets or seed retirement. |\n| Authoritative completion | All six requirements, completed dependencies, applicable uniform backlog gates and fresh stable-revision migration evidence independently accepted. | Features, targets or release claims outside the recorded support set. |\n\nBefore marking any SH-03 or later milestone complete:\n\n1. Map every numbered requirement and required variant to its owning contract,\n   implementation entrypoint, accepted `.gravity`/`.qst` fixture pair,\n   rejection-family fixtures, expected artifact/result or diagnostic, and\n   actual selected checks. Missing cases are open work, not implicit passes.\n2. Execute the claimed Gravity implementation. Classifying supplied records,\n   emitting a report, interpreting an unrelated host implementation, or\n   compiling source without exercising the claimed behavior is insufficient.\n3. Preserve or explicitly transform the pass's required source, origin,\n   binding, type, effect, capability, ownership, safety and artifact facts.\n   Test malformed outputs and relevant stale/substituted boundary inputs.\n4. Pass focused checks and affected integration/regressions; preserve failed,\n   interrupted, skipped and unsupported results accurately. A target emission\n   claim additionally needs an independent target tool's validation/execution.\n5. At explicit admission, satisfy BOOT3/TEST13 stage comparison, conformance,\n   source/compiler/dependency provenance, BOOT5 support and TCB accounting,\n   unsafe audits and required independent review for that migration. SH-26\n   through SH-29 additionally require their whole-stage/release checks.\n6. Record an accepted clean verified revision and evidence references in the\n   existing backlog/compatibility records. Keep implementation, integration\n   and authoritative status separate. A stale proof stays stale after edits;\n   it does not prohibit focused testing of those edits.\n\nSH-00 through SH-02 retain their already accepted baseline/process/envelope\nscope. They do not acquire new language obligations from this document.\nRecorded completion is not reopened merely to reissue unchanged evidence.\nReuse valid unchanged expensive evidence; refresh affected evidence when\nsource, compiler, semantic inputs, target, dependencies or policy invalidate it.\nPreserve existing resource limits and admission requirements for heavy runs.\n\n",
  "e746b7258bc661ab9fb8dbdcac47de1cd2e902bfd0865cc534a83497c94fc5bf"
  "- [ ] `evolution/namespace-decision` Govern reservation, transfer and abandoned namespace takeover\n\nStatus: Open\n\nSource requirement: `governance/namespace-decision`\n\nRequired outputs: Owned namespace reservation/transfer/takeover decision and owner-verification audit artifact\n\nClosing evidence: Apply declared namespace and reserved-name policy, verify the actual owner for transfer, and retain reason/decision/history for reservation, dispute or abandoned-package takeover. Publish audit references usable by registries/package policy without exposing private namespace information or treating identity lookup as authorization. Actual complete accepted artifact and consumer result for the named source contract, with input/source/policy/tool identity. Historical stage0 records or a passing schema alone do not implement these outcomes.\n\nRequired rejection evidence: Reject transfer without verified ownership and audit record, reserved-name abuse, standard-library impersonation and unmitigated dependency-confusion risk. Do not infer an automatic takeover interval or let Build/Auth mechanics adjudicate governance policy. Malformed/missing/stale/wrong-scope records and each named source rejection produce the original GOV diagnostics at the actual consuming boundary.\n\nDependencies: GOV10 registry identity/ownership policy; PKG9 namespace/private-registry facts; GOV4 supply-chain review when relevant. Dependencies are required artifacts/conditional consumers, not a claim their producer tasks are implemented or a cyclic all-tasks-complete gate.\n\nExact source criteria: facet/bootstrap-governance/5278996755efd7c635deddf8, facet/bootstrap-governance/edcfd7d4957751f76b5655b8, facet/bootstrap-governance/9b8c71fd62a70ea16d27b359, facet/bootstrap-governance/69c0d07980e8f00b73c8ec9e, facet/bootstrap-governance/725b6496a1c3494ae47c66b8, facet/bootstrap-governance/961b6f80528a574c47900129, facet/bootstrap-governance/0ff8c58df9a54b696418b4cd, facet/bootstrap-governance/0ec4